## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP with partitioning and additive segment costs; uses prefix sums to optimize the transition to O(nk).

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitioning into k segments
- Prefix sums to compute segment costs in O(1)
- State transition optimization with a running max and backpointers
- Reconstruction of cut points

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPartitionScore(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  if (k <= 0 || k > n) return {score: 0, cuts: []};
  const diff = new Array(n-1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n-1; i++) diff[i] = Math.abs(A[i+1] - A[i]);
  const pref = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = pref[i-1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    let best = -Infinity, bestP = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const p = i - 1;
      const val = dp[p][t-1] - pref[p];
      if (val > best) { best = val; bestP = p; }
      dp[i][t] = pref[i-1] + best;
      take[i][t] = bestP;
    }
  }
  let cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = take[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = p;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to require exactly k edits instead of at most?
- How to reduce space to O(n*k)?
- How to reconstruct the edited string and the subsequence?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests proficiency with partition dynamic programming where subarrays must be non-overlapping and values derive from range statistics. The solution requires careful separation of concerns: precomputing range queries, implementing the DP transition, and reconstructing the solution through pointer tracking.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping contiguous subarrays as partition elements
- Dynamic programming with two-dimensional state tracking
- Range minima preprocessing for O(1) query access
- Solution reconstruction through backpointers
- Complexity optimization through careful algorithm design

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysMin(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;

  // Precompute range minima
  const mins = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let m = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      m = Math.min(m, A[j]);
      mins[i][j] = m;
    }
  }
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a sophisticated dynamic programming variant where the non-adjacency constraint introduces complexity beyond standard interval selection problems. It requires careful state design and transition logic to maintain separation between blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming with separation constraints between selected intervals
- Efficient computation of range maxima for subarray value calculation
- Backpointer implementation for reconstructing optimal block endpoints
- Time-space trade-off analysis: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space when using precomputed range maximum queries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Precompute range maxima
  const rangeMax = Array(n).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let currentMax = A[i];
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      currentMax = Math.max(currentMax, A[j]);
      rangeMax[i][j] = currentMax;
    }
  }

  // Base case: 0 blocks
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  // Fill DP table
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      }

      // End block at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i-1; s++) {
        const prevIdx = s-2 >= 0 ? s-2 : 0;
        if (dp[prevIdx][t-1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = rangeMax[s-1][i-1] + dp[prevIdx][t-1];
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = s-1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], reconstruct: () => reconstructBlocks(prev, n, k) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How can we optimize the time complexity to O(nk) using a monotonic queue or segment tree structure?
- How does the solution adapt to different indexing conventions (1-based vs 0-based) and handle edge cases where n < 2k-1?
- What modifications are needed if we allow adjacent blocks or change the objective function?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over time with multiple constraints: multiple transactions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Two DP states: cash (not holding) and hold (holding).
- Transitions model buy/sell with transaction count t.
- Base cases initialize day 0; backtracking recovers trade days.
- Space optimization possible to O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfit(prices, k){
  const n = prices.length;
  if (n===0 || k===0) return 0;
  const cash = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const hold = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) cash[0][t] = (t===0)?0:-1e15;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++){
      cash[i][t] = cash[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) cash[i][t] = Math.max(cash[i][t], hold[i-1][t] + prices[i-1]);
      hold[i][t] = hold[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i][t], cash[i-1][t-1] - prices[i-1]);
    }
  }
  let best = 0;
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) best = Math.max(best, cash[n][t]);
  return best;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the exact trade days from the DP table?
- How does the solution change if k >= n/2 (unlimited transactions)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a nontrivial DP for partitioning with a per-block cost derived from a range statistic, plus reconstruction across k blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute cost[l][r] = max(A[l..r])*(r-l+1) in O(n^2).
- DP: dp[i][t] = max_{l≤i} dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i].
- Track par[i][t] for block endpoints; base dp[i][1] = cost[0][i].
- Space/time tradeoffs; discuss optimizations (rolling arrays, divide-and-conquer). 

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, a[r]);
      cost[l][r] = m * (r - l + 1);
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + cost[l][i];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a faster optimization (e.g., divide-and-conquer) if opt indices are monotone?
- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks or variable block values (e.g., using different per-block metrics)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a DP with a non-trivial cost function and prefix optimization.

## Key Concepts
- DP on prefixes and partitions
- O(1) substring cost via precomputed diff/pref
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// Precompute diff and prefix
let diff = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) diff[i] = s[i-1] !== s[i-2] ? 1:0;
let pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i];

// cost(a,b)
function cost(a,b){ return pref[b] - pref[a]; }

// DP
let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
let par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = cost(1,i); }

for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
  for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
    for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
      let val = dp[p][t-1] + cost(p+1,i);
      if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How could you optimize to O(nk) with divide-and-conquer DP if cost satisfies quadrangle inequality?
- How would you extract the actual partition end indices from par?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a non-trivial DP with a non-additive cost function and reconstruction, plus a precomputation layer for inversions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix length i and blocks t with transition from a previous breakpoint j
- invCost(l,r) as block cost (inversions within subarray)
- Reconstruction pointers for block boundaries
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk) or O(nk) with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function precomputeInv(a){
  const n=a.length; const inv=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let cnt=0;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      for(let i=l;i<r;i++) if(a[i]>a[r]) cnt++;
      inv[l][r]=cnt;
    }
  }
  return inv;
}
```

